  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain likely Thursday night, mainly Downeast. \nLittle to no drought improvement anticipated.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nRain Thursday night Downeast will be coming ahead of an\napproaching cold front and approaching upper level trough, with\na weak surface low pressure developing over southern New\nHampshire and tracking east just offshore Thursday night. There\nis still some uncertainty in just how far north the steadier \nrain makes it, but we do have pretty high confidence that the \nhighest rain totals will be from Bangor south. Totals could \nreach around a half inch, with the best chance of this occurring\nbeing right on the coast. North of Bangor, less than a tenth of\nan inch is expected, with amounts tapering to near zero in the \nfar north. This is a quick-hitting system, with the steady rain\nending by about sunrise Friday. The far north could have a few\nshowers Friday morning/midday as the cooler air moves in as\nwell, but totals from this will generally be less than a tenth\nof an inch, if any. Overall, little if any improvement in the\ndrought conditions expected with this system.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Gusty winds from the northwest during the day \nFriday could lead to isolated power outages.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nCooler air moving in, with a strong pressure gradient, will lead\nto gusty winds over land during the day Friday. Anticipating \nmax gusts 30-40 mph, with about an equal threat areawide. This\nwill be strong enough to lead to isolated power outages. The \nprimary threat is during the day Friday from mid-morning to \nsunset.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Another chance for rain showers this weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nA weak low pressure system sliding Eastward across Southern New\nEngland this weekend will bring our next chance for rain \nshowers. Timing and track of the system remains uncertain at \nthis time with some guidance delaying the onset of rain until \nwell into Sunday or takes the system Eastward into the Gulf of\nMaine. Regardless, minimal drought relief is expected with \nexpected rainfall totals below 0.2 inches across the region with\nhighest totals Downeast.",
